==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{root|ang|ine-pro|*bʰruHg-}} {{dercat|ang|gem-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*brūkan||to enjoy, use}}.

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Cognate with {{cog|ofs|brūka}}, {{cog|osx|brūkan}}, {{cog|odt|*brūkan}}, {{cog|goh|brūhhan}}, {{cog|got|𐌱𐍂𐌿𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽}}. The Indo-European root was also the source of Latin {{m|la|fruor||enjoy}}.{{rel-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|brūcan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|brūcan}} ''(+ genitive)''

# to [[use]]
# to [[enjoy]]
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=xtM0rOEXqJIC&pg=PA48 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius’]] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Uton nū '''brūcan''' þisses undernmetes swā þā sċulon þe heora ǣfenġifl on helle ġefeċċan sċulon.''|Now let's '''enjoy''' this breakfast as comrades who must fetch their dinner in hell.}}
# to [[harness]], take advantage of, [[employ]], [[exploit]]

